<mujoco model="panda pick and place (LIBERO-matched table + camera)">
  <!-- LIBERO-matched scene for libero/libero_closed_loop.py.

       WHY meshdir IS AN EXPLICIT RELATIVE PATH: MuJoCo resolves meshdir against the
       directory of the TOP-LEVEL file given to from_xml_path(), not against the file
       that declares it. This scene lives in scenes/ and the meshes live in the
       mujoco_menagerie submodule, so meshdir must spell out the way back:
       ../../mujoco_menagerie/franka_emika_panda/assets. It used to say just "assets",
       which forced these files to sit inside the submodule -- untracked, with no
       history, and one silent revert of the arm gains as a result.

       WHY IT INCLUDES panda.xml AND NOT scene.xml: scene.xml puts its ground plane at
       z = 0, which is exactly where panda.xml puts link0. LIBERO needs the floor 0.912 m
       BELOW the robot base (see below), so the floor has to move -- hence scene.xml's
       ~20 lines of visual/asset/light are reproduced here with a relocated plane.

       ===================================================================
       GROUND TRUTH (read out of robosuite 1.5.2, not guessed)
       ===================================================================
       Panda.base_xpos_offset["table"](0.8)  = (-0.56, 0, 0)
       Panda.default_base                    = "RethinkMount"
       RobotModel.bottom_offset              = (0, 0, -0.912)
       set_base_xpos => link0 = pos - bottom_offset
         => link0 world pos                  = (-0.56, 0, 0.912)
       TableArena table_offset               = (0, 0, 0.8)   [table TOP]
       table_full_size                       = (0.8, 0.8, 0.05)
       Panda.init_qpos = (0, 0.19635, 0, -2.61799, 0, 2.94159, 0.78540)

       The consequence, and the thing that was wrong before: LIBERO's Panda is NOT
       floor-mounted. It sits on a 0.912 m pedestal, so its base is 0.112 m ABOVE the
       table top and the arm works DOWNWARD onto a surface just below itself. Our scene
       had the arm bolted at z = 0 reaching DOWN to objects at z = 0.02 -- so every
       eef_pos we reported was ~0.8 m below anything in the checkpoint's training
       distribution, and the model responded by commanding descent until the arm bottomed
       out on the floor.

       ===================================================================
       THIS FILE'S FRAME: world origin AT THE ROBOT BASE (link0 = 0,0,0)
       ===================================================================
       We cannot move link0 -- it is fixed at the origin by panda.xml, and editing that
       would change the DROID path and invalidate the collected dataset. So instead of
       raising the robot to LIBERO's 0.912, we lower everything else by the same amount.
       The RELATIVE geometry -- which is all the physics and the arm's posture depend on
       -- is then identical to LIBERO:

         quantity                     LIBERO frame     this file     delta
         link0                        z = 0.912        z =  0.0      -0.912
         table top                    z = 0.900        z = -0.012    -0.912
         ground                       z = 0.000        z = -0.912    -0.912
         table centre (x)             x = 0.0          x =  0.60     +0.60
         eef at LIBERO reset          z = 1.173        z =  0.261    -0.912

       CORRECTED 2026-07-28. The table top used to sit at -0.112, i.e. 0.112 m below
       link0, on the reading that LIBERO's table is at 0.800 with link0 at 0.912. A
       live LIBERO env says otherwise: link0 0.912, table top 0.900, so the surface is
       only 0.012 m below the base. Our table was 100 mm too LOW -- every descent the
       policy commanded fell short of the surface it expected, which is the single
       most likely cause of it continuing to press downward into the table.

       Absolute coordinates therefore differ from LIBERO by a constant
       (+0.56, 0, -0.912). Since the model conditions on absolute eef_pos, the loop adds
       (-0.56, 0, +0.912) back before sending state -- a pure change of origin, not a
       fudge. See LIBERO_ORIGIN_OFFSET in libero_closed_loop.py.
  -->
  <include file="panda_robotiq.xml"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="120" elevation="-20"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7" rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <texture type="2d" name="groundplane" builtin="checker" mark="edge" rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.2 0.3"
      markrgb="0.8 0.8 0.8" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="groundplane" texture="groundplane" texuniform="true" texrepeat="5 5" reflectance="0.2"/>

    <!-- Table top kept light and matte, like robosuite's ceramic table material. -->
    <material name="table_top_mat" rgba="0.85 0.82 0.78 1" specular="0.1" shininess="0.1"/>
    <material name="pedestal_mat" rgba="0.25 0.25 0.28 1"/>

    <!-- Prefixed obj_ because panda.xml already defines a material named "green"
         (a status-LED mesh) -- reusing it raises "repeated name 'green' in material". -->
    <material name="obj_red" rgba="0.8 0.1 0.1 1"/>
    <material name="obj_green" rgba="0.1 0.7 0.2 1"/>
    <material name="obj_blue" rgba="0.1 0.3 0.8 1"/>
    <material name="obj_yellow" rgba="0.85 0.75 0.1 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>

    <!-- Ground 0.912 m below the robot base, matching RethinkMount's height. -->
    <geom name="floor" pos="0 0 -0.912" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="groundplane"/>

    <!-- The pedestal the robot stands on. Purely cosmetic/structural: fills the gap
         between the relocated floor and link0 so the arm isn't floating in mid-air, and
         gives roughly the visual mass of robosuite's RethinkMount pedestal. -->
    <body name="pedestal" pos="0 0 -0.456">
      <geom type="box" size="0.18 0.18 0.456" material="pedestal_mat"/>
    </body>

    <!-- TABLE. robosuite's static LIBERO scenes model it as one solid box of half-size
         (0.4, 0.4, 0.4) whose top face is the work surface, so that is reproduced here
         rather than TableArena's thin-top-plus-legs variant (same surface, fewer geoms,
         and no legs for the arm to catch on).
           half-size (0.4, 0.4, 0.45), centre z = -0.462
           => top face at -0.462 + 0.45 = -0.012   (LIBERO's 0.900, measured)
           => bottom still at -0.912, i.e. resting exactly on the floor
         Centred 0.56 m in front of the base in x, so the near edge is 0.16 m away --
         the same base-to-table gap LIBERO has (base -0.6, table spans -0.5..0.5). -->
    <body name="table" pos="0.56 0 -0.462">
      <geom name="table_geom" type="box" size="0.4 0.4 0.45" material="table_top_mat" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Objects now rest ON THE TABLE (top at z = -0.012) rather than on the floor.
         Sphere centres sit one radius above the surface; bins sit flat on it. -->

    <body name="red_box" pos="0.56 -0.28 0.008">
      <freejoint/>
      <geom name="red_box_geom" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.02" material="obj_red" mass="0.05"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The pick target. A 40 mm CUBE as of 2026-08-03, replacing the 40 mm sphere that was
         here before: half-extent 0.02, the same 40 mm across, well inside the Robotiq
         2F-85's ~85mm opening. Rationale in libero/PROGRESS.md sec.26.
         Position here is only a default -- the loop overwrites this freejoint per episode. -->
    <body name="green_box" pos="0.56 0 0.008">
      <freejoint/>
      <!-- CONTACT PARAMS (2026-07-28). Inherited from the sphere and DELIBERATELY KEPT even
           though the reasoning that produced them no longer holds. That reasoning was: a
           40 mm sphere between two flat pads has no form closure, it is held by friction
           alone, and with menagerie's default contact softness a sustained squeeze extrudes
           it -- traced tick by tick, the fingers seated correctly at 0.0195 m for ~17 ticks,
           then drove on through to 0.0000 and the ball shot out sideways, so every demo
           lifted the ball and then dropped it 0.10-0.12 m short of the bin.
           A cube has flat faces and therefore form closure. Params held fixed so the box
           scene is one variable away from the ball scene; retune only against a measurement.
             priority 2   outranks the pads' priority 1, so THESE params win the contact
             condim 4     torsional friction; load-bearing for a sphere, redundant for a cube
             solref/solimp  stiff and near-rigid, so the squeeze stops at the surface
                          instead of slowly sinking into it -- still earns its place -->
      <geom name="green_box_geom" type="box" size="0.02 0.02 0.02" material="obj_green" mass="0.05"
        priority="2" condim="4" friction="2 0.05 0.0001"
        solref="0.004 1" solimp="0.99 0.999 0.001"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Bins: open-top trays, 5 box geoms each (flat bottom + 4 thin walls), a
         10x10x4 cm tray centred on the body origin. Placed on the table surface, kept
         inside its 0.8x0.8 m extent (x in [0.16, 0.96], y in [-0.4, 0.4]). -->
    <body name="green_bin" pos="0.56 0.25 -0.012">
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.05 0.002" pos="0 0 0.002" material="obj_green"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_green"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="-0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_green"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 0.05 0.02" material="obj_green"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 -0.05 0.02" material="obj_green"/>
    </body>

    <body name="blue_bin" pos="0.56 -0.25 -0.012">
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.05 0.002" pos="0 0 0.002" material="obj_blue"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_blue"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="-0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_blue"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 0.05 0.02" material="obj_blue"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 -0.05 0.02" material="obj_blue"/>
    </body>

    <body name="yellow_bin" pos="0.80 0 -0.012">
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.05 0.002" pos="0 0 0.002" material="obj_yellow"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_yellow"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.002 0.05 0.02" pos="-0.05 0 0.02" material="obj_yellow"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 0.05 0.02" material="obj_yellow"/>
      <geom type="box" size="0.05 0.002 0.02" pos="0 -0.05 0.02" material="obj_yellow"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Kept so code that looks the body up still works, though external_cam below no
         longer targets it (it is a rigid mount now, like LIBERO's). -->
    <body name="scene_center" pos="0.56 0 -0.012"/>

    <!-- external_cam == LIBERO's `agentview`.
         LIBERO/robosuite (identical in every libero_*_base_style.xml and robosuite's
         table_arena.xml):
             <camera mode="fixed" name="agentview" pos="0.5 0 1.35"
                     quat="0.653 0.271 0.271 0.653"/>
         no fovy attribute -> MuJoCo's default 45 degrees. With the table top at 0.800
         that is an offset of (+0.5, 0, +0.55) from the table centre: 0.743 m standoff,
         ~45 degrees below horizon, dead-on from the front, framing ~0.60 m.

         Reproduced here as the same offset from OUR table centre (0.56, 0, -0.012):
             pos = (0.56 + 0.5, 0, -0.012 + 0.55) = (1.06, 0, 0.538)
         (raised with the table on 2026-07-28 so the framing of the work surface is
         unchanged. NOTE agentview is per-scene in LIBERO, not canonical: measured
         link0-relative it is (1.497, 0, 0.650) in libero_object and (1.319, 0, 0.698)
         in libero_spatial, with different pitches. There is no single right answer to
         match, so this keeps the framing sec.11 measured the 16 px ball at.)
         -> view direction (-0.5, 0, -0.55) normalised = (-0.673, 0, -0.740).

         mode="targetbody" is deliberately gone (LIBERO's is mode="fixed").

         xyaxes was NOT hand-derived -- an earlier hand-derivation of the wrist camera got
         both axes negated, a 180-degree roll that renders upside down while every
         forward-vector check still passes. These are verified by rendering. -->
    <camera name="external_cam" pos="1.06 0 0.538" fovy="45"
            xyaxes="0 1 0  -0.74033 0 0.67224"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
